---
// VISUAILS — Terms of Service. Ported from the SvelteKit terms page (itself
// ported from static /terms.html). Legal pages are deliberately
// plain/scannable, not "designed" — no reveal animations or kickers, per the
// rebuild brief.
//
// Three places are no longer the ported copy:
//
//   §2  A "reserved order" is defined, so §9's deposit clause has a defined
//       term to attach to rather than a marketing word.
//
//       IT USED TO DEFINE "DROP" — "a single order covering a whole collection
//       or launch, rather than individual products" — and that definition died
//       with the packages in August 2026 (section 0 of src/data/pricing.js).
//       Two things were wrong with it under the new model, and the second is
//       the one that mattered in a contract: the word means a collection going
//       live to the brands we sell to, so a contract that redefines it is
//       redefining their word; and "rather than individual products" drew a
//       line the price list no longer draws, because every count is priced now.
//       The line the site DOES draw is WINDOW_THRESHOLD, which is exactly what
//       §6 and §9 need a defined term for — one is about the window, the other
//       about the deposit that reserves it.
//
//   §7  "Your files & how long we keep them" — new, per AUDIT-TASK-0.md:171.
//       The same clocks as the Privacy Policy's §6, stated here because a
//       retention promise a client can rely on belongs in the contract and not
//       only in the privacy notice. There are three of them, and they are not
//       the same number twice: source material 90 days, delivered visuals 12
//       months, and the private order link 90 days from order close. That last
//       one is the one to be careful with — this section used to fold it into
//       the 12-month line and say the visuals "and the link you download them
//       from" both last a year, which the portal has never done. The reasoning,
//       and the code that settles it, is in privacy.astro's header. Sections
//       7–12 shifted to 8–13. It also carries the first link from /terms to
//       /privacy; the two documents did not reference each other at all before.
//
//   §9  gains the 50/50 deposit paragraph, per AUDIT-TASK-0.md:128. Scoped to
//       what is invoiced rather than charged at checkout, so a free
//       founding-offer order does not fall into a clause about instalments.
//
// §6 AND §10 — the flagged decision, now taken.
//
// §6 used to read "We target delivery in around 24 hours from a complete brief.
// Each order includes up to 3 revision rounds." Both halves promised the whole
// customer base something only one tier could deliver: a single-product order
// runs in the standard queue at 2–4 working days and, since the client's
// decision, no order carries a round count at all. This was the last unretired
// 24-hour claim on the site and the only contractually binding one, which is
// why task #190 could not close until it was settled.
//
// Three changes follow from that:
//
//   §6  is now "Delivery" and nothing else. Both timing statements are read
//       from turnaround() rather than typed, so the contract cannot drift from
//       the tier model the way it just did. It states explicitly that we do not
//       name a date for an individual product — the brief's standing rule is
//       never to promise a delivery date the capacity gate has not cleared, and
//       a contract is the worst possible place to break it.
//
//   §10 absorbs what §6 used to promise, in its new form: we ask on every order
//       whether the client is happy, and if not we agree the resolution with
//       them. This is the ONLY place on the site that enumerates the three
//       remedies (revision, refund, credit on the next order). See the block
//       comment in src/data/pricing.js for why they appear here and nowhere
//       else. §10's old sentence — "we will use your included revision rounds
//       to put it right" — pointed at an entitlement that no longer exists.
//
//   §4  no longer types its prices. €39.99 and €59.99 were the last two typed
//       figures anywhere on the site; everything else reads from pricing.js,
//       and the one file where a stale price is actually enforceable was the
//       one file still holding its own copy. The add-on bullet went with them:
//       it described optional extras "shown before checkout", and the three
//       checkout add-ons (multi-format export, 4K, priority) were removed from
//       /pricing. What is left sits on top of an order and is quoted, not sold
//       at a checkout step.
//
//       §4 ALSO STOPPED QUOTING A FLAT RATE. It printed AMOUNT.catalog and
//       AMOUNT.lifestyle as if each were the price of a product; they are the
//       ENTRY RUNG of a ladder now, and a contract that names one figure for
//       something priced on a sliding scale is the worst version of that
//       mistake. Both ends are named, the fall is stated, and /pricing is
//       linked for the rungs in between — which is also where a client goes to
//       check the figure they were quoted.
//
// AUDIT-TASK-0.md:128's capacity/delivery-window wording is what §6 now
// carries. It was blocked on this decision and is no longer blocked.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import {
  euro, turnaround, ladderRate, ladderFloor, LADDER, WINDOW_THRESHOLD,
} from '../data/pricing.js';

const t0 = turnaround('unattended', 'en').toLowerCase();
const t1 = turnaround('attended', 'en').toLowerCase();

// The two ends of each ladder §4 quotes, and the count the floor starts at.
// Read, never typed — the one file on the site where a stale price is actually
// enforceable is the last file that should hold its own copy of one.
const FLOOR_FROM = LADDER.catalog[LADDER.catalog.length - 1][0];
---

<Layout
  title="Terms of Service — VISUAILS"
  description="The terms governing your use of VISUAILS — AI-assisted, human-reviewed product visuals. Orders, pricing, usage rights, payment, VAT and Dutch law."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow-page">Legal</span>
      <h1 class="display" style="font-size:clamp(2.4rem,5vw,4rem)">Terms of Service</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="margin-top:1.2rem">The terms that apply when you order product visuals from VISUAILS. Please read them before placing an order.</p>
      <p class="legal-meta">Last updated: July 2026</p>
      <p class="legal-note">This is a general template — review with a qualified legal professional before relying on it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container narrow legal">

      <h2>1. Introduction &amp; acceptance</h2>
      <p>These Terms of Service govern your use of VISUAILS and any order you place with us. VISUAILS is operated from Enschede, the Netherlands (KVK 99742993, VAT NL005407575B96). By placing an order, requesting a test sample or otherwise using our service, you agree to these terms. If you do not agree, please do not use the service.</p>

      <h2>2. Definitions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>"We", "us", "VISUAILS"</strong> — the provider of the service.</li>
        <li><strong>"You", "client"</strong> — the individual or business ordering visuals.</li>
        <li><strong>"Visual"</strong> — an image or video we produce for you.</li>
        <li><strong>"Source material"</strong> — the product photos, images and information you provide.</li>
        <li><strong>"Order"</strong> — a request for one or more visuals under these terms.</li>
        <li><strong>"Reserved order"</strong> — an order of {WINDOW_THRESHOLD} products or more, which is booked into our capacity calendar and given a reserved delivery window.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>3. The service</h2>
      <p>VISUAILS produces AI-assisted, human-reviewed product visuals — catalog images, lifestyle scenes, and video — from the source material you supply. Every visual is generated through our pipeline and checked by a person before delivery. We aim for consistent, publish-ready results, but visuals are creative work and exact outcomes can vary.</p>

      <h2>4. Orders &amp; pricing</h2>
      <p>Visuals are priced per product, and the rate falls as the number of products in the order rises. Indicative rates are:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Catalog</strong> — {euro(ladderRate('catalog', 1), 'en')} per product, falling to {euro(ladderFloor('catalog'), 'en')} from {FLOOR_FROM} products up. A set of four photos (front, back, a detail close-up and one on-model shot).</li>
        <li><strong>Lifestyle visuals</strong> — {euro(ladderRate('lifestyle', 1), 'en')} per product, falling to {euro(ladderFloor('lifestyle'), 'en')} from {FLOOR_FROM} products up. A three-photo carousel.</li>
        <li><strong>The rate between those ends</strong> — set out in full on our <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a>. The rate that applies is the one for the total number of products in your order, and it applies to every product in it.</li>
        <li><strong>Video, custom work and monthly plans</strong> — quoted individually before you commit.</li>
        <li><strong>Brand Model</strong> — added on top of an order and quoted in advance. It is not sold at checkout.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>New businesses can request one test sample per business, so you can judge our quality before ordering. Prices are confirmed at the time of order. Unless stated otherwise, prices are exclusive of VAT where applicable.</p>

      <h2>5. Your responsibilities</h2>
      <p>You are responsible for the source material you upload. By submitting it, you confirm that:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>You own the rights to the product photos and images, or have permission to use them.</li>
        <li>The content does not infringe anyone's intellectual property, privacy or other rights.</li>
        <li>The content is not illegal, harmful, misleading or otherwise prohibited.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>We may decline or stop work on any order that appears to breach these conditions.</p>

      <h2>6. Delivery</h2>
      <p>How long an order takes depends on how you order. Individual products run in the standard queue: {t0} from a complete brief. We do not name a delivery date for an individual product, because a date we have not reserved capacity for is not a date. A reserved order runs in {t1}, and it holds that place while smaller orders move around it.</p>
      <p>Times can still shift with the size and complexity of an order. If a delivery is going to move, we tell you — we would rather say so than let a date pass in silence.</p>

      <h2>7. Your files &amp; how long we keep them</h2>
      <p>Three periods are fixed, and they are deliberately different lengths:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Source material</strong> — the photos and files you upload are deleted 90 days after your order closes. If you need us to keep them longer, ask in writing before then and we will agree it with you.</li>
        <li><strong>Delivered visuals</strong> — we keep your finished visuals for 12 months from delivery. A delivery outlives the material it was made from.</li>
        <li><strong>Your order link</strong> — the private link you download from works for 90 days after your order closes, then it stops. If you need the files after that, email us and we will send a new link.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Keep your own copy of anything you rely on: after 12 months we cannot guarantee a re-download. How we handle your personal data more generally, including how that private link is protected, is set out in our <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.</p>

      <h2>8. Intellectual property &amp; usage rights</h2>
      <p>You retain all rights to your own product and brand assets — the source material you provide. On full payment, you receive commercial usage rights to the delivered visuals, so you can use them across your webshop, marketplaces, ads and social channels. We may retain examples of our work for our portfolio unless you ask us in writing not to.</p>

      <h2>9. Payment &amp; VAT</h2>
      <p>Payment is handled through our payment provider. Invoices are issued for your records. For EU business customers with a valid VAT number, the reverse-charge mechanism may apply where the conditions are met; otherwise Dutch VAT rules apply. You are responsible for providing accurate billing and VAT details.</p>
      <p>Small orders and test samples are paid in full at checkout. A reserved order or custom work is invoiced rather than charged at checkout, in two parts: 50% on confirmation, which is what reserves your production slot, and 50% before the finished files are released. Work does not start, and no slot is held, until the first instalment is paid.</p>

      <h2>10. Cancellations, refunds and putting it right</h2>
      <p>Because visuals are produced to your specific brief, work that has already started or been delivered generally cannot be cancelled for a refund.</p>
      <p>We ask on every order whether you are happy with what you received. If you are not, tell us what is wrong and we will go through it with you. What we agree from there depends on the problem, and can be a revision, a refund, or credit against your next order. We would rather understand what you actually wanted than work through a fixed number of rounds.</p>
      <p>What we agree relates to the brief we worked from; a substantially new direction is a new order rather than a correction. Refunds are at our reasonable discretion and assessed case by case.</p>


      <h2>10a. Right of withdrawal for consumers</h2>
      <p><em>This section is a draft. Have it reviewed by a lawyer before relying on it.</em></p>
      <p>If you order as a consumer — that is, not in the course of a profession or business — a distance contract normally gives you fourteen days to withdraw. Two statutory exceptions apply to our service, and they are set out here because you need to know them before you order.</p>
      <p><strong>Made to your specification.</strong> Every visual is produced from the product photographs you supply and to the brief you give us. It is therefore made to your specifications and clearly personalised, and for that reason falls outside the right of withdrawal.</p>
      <p><strong>A service fully performed.</strong> When you place your order we ask you to expressly confirm that we may begin straight away, and to declare that you lose your right of withdrawal once the order has been delivered. We record that confirmation against your order, together with the wording you were shown at the time. Without it we do not start.</p>
      <p>As long as nothing has been delivered and you have not given that confirmation, you may withdraw within the cooling-off period at no cost. If you have already paid, you get that money back. Email hello@visuails.com — that is enough, no form is needed.</p>
      <p>All of this applies to consumers only. If you order on behalf of a business the right of withdrawal does not apply, and section 10 and the rest of these terms govern instead.</p>

      <h2>11. Liability limitations</h2>
      <p>We provide the service with reasonable skill and care, but to the extent permitted by law we are not liable for indirect or consequential losses, lost profits, or losses arising from your use of the visuals. Our total liability for any order is limited to the amount you paid for that order. Nothing in these terms excludes liability that cannot be excluded by law.</p>

      <h2>12. Governing law</h2>
      <p>These terms are governed by the laws of the Netherlands. Any disputes will be subject to the jurisdiction of the competent Dutch courts, unless mandatory law provides otherwise.</p>

      <h2>13. Contact</h2>
      <p>Questions about these terms? Email <a href="mailto:hello@visuails.com">hello@visuails.com</a> or message us on WhatsApp at <a href="https://wa.me/31625436130" target="_blank" rel="noopener">+31 6 25436130</a>.</p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="cta-band">
        <h2 class="display" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,3.6rem)">Ready when you are.</h2>
        <p class="lead" style="margin:1.2rem auto 0;text-align:center">Start with a test sample, or place your first order. We'll confirm scope and pricing before any work begins.</p>
        <div class="flex" style="justify-content:center;margin-top:2rem">
          <a href="/test-sample" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Test sample</a>
          <a href="/start" class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg">Start an order</a>
          <a href="https://wa.me/31625436130?text=Hi%20VISUAILS%2C%20I%20have%20a%20question%20about%20your%20terms." class="btn btn-wa" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            <svg class="i" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M21 11.5a8.38 8.38 0 0 1-8.5 8.5 8.5 8.5 0 0 1-4-1L3 20l1.5-4.5A8.38 8.38 0 0 1 3.5 11 8.5 8.5 0 0 1 12 3a8.38 8.38 0 0 1 8.5 8.5z" /></svg>
            WhatsApp
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Legal-text typography — page-scoped, plain and scannable by design. */
  .narrow { max-width: 820px; }
  .legal h2 { margin-top: 2.6rem; font-size: 1.5rem; }
  .legal h3 { margin-top: 1.6rem; font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--ink); }
  .legal p, .legal li { color: var(--ink-2); line-height: 1.7; }
  .legal p { margin-top: 0.9rem; }
  .legal ul { margin-top: 0.9rem; padding-left: 1.2rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem; }
  .legal ul li { list-style: disc; }
  .legal a { color: var(--accent-bright); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px; }
  .legal-note { margin-top: 1.6rem; padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem; border: 1px solid var(--line-strong); font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .legal-meta { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-3); margin-top: 0.4rem; }
</style>
